hEDS Musculoskeletal Phenotype
Complex Fatigue Entities
Severe joint instability and micro-traumas

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-020
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of acute, severe joint pain and immobility to rule out frank joint dislocation or neurovascular compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported multiple sudden, painful joint "pops" followed by severe muscle spasm and inability to move the left shoulder and right knee. Immediate on-site assessment was mandated to rule out complete dislocation requiring closed reduction and to manage severe pain crisis before regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My shoulder and knee both slipped out of place, and the muscle spasms are locking my body in absolute agony.
History of Present Illness: Patient with hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome presents with an acute exacerbation of severe joint instability. Over the past 4 hours, minor movement resulted in acute subluxations of the left glenohumeral joint and right patella. While the joints spontaneously reduced, the patient is left with severe chronic myofascial pain and intense reactive muscle spasm. Patient notes a history of early-onset osteoarthritis in these joints.
Functional Status: Immobilized on the floor/couch due to severe spasm and fear of further subluxation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to safely transfer to bed or reach kitchen for water.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Distressed, holding left arm securely against chest, right leg held in slight flexion.
Physical Exam: Left shoulder is located; no gross deformity. Severe secondary spasm of the left trapezius and deltoid. Right patella is centrally tracking but exquisitely tender over the medial retinaculum with a mild effusion. Distal pulses and neurology (sensation, motor function of fingers/toes) are completely intact bilaterally. Beighton score confirmed 8/9. Presentation maps to severe joint instability and micro-traumas.
Environmental Observations: Patient was assessed on the living room floor; assisted to the couch post-examination.
Medication Reconciliation: No effective analgesia currently available in the home.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: hEDS Musculoskeletal Phenotype - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Frank joint dislocation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal anatomical contour of the joints on examination, intact range of motion (though limited by pain), and no neurovascular deficit.

Acute ligamentous rupture (e.g., ACL) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Stable Lachman and anterior drawer tests, though chronic laxity is noted.

Acute septic arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, multi-joint involvement mechanically linked to movement, and lack of intense heat/erythema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden onset of severe mechanical joint pain and subsequent immobility required an urgent physical examination to rule out persistent frank dislocation, which would constitute an orthopedic emergency requiring immediate reduction to prevent avascular necrosis or nerve damage. Having clinically confirmed that the joints were subluxed but spontaneously reduced, the immediate medical requirement was to administer potent antispasmodic and anti-inflammatory medications to break the severe, functionally limiting secondary muscle spasm.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered single dose of Diazepam 5mg orally to release acute protective muscle spasms. Prescribed Meloxicam 15mg daily for micro-trauma inflammation. Targeted intervention strategy includes Vitamin C, COX-2 inhibitors, and low dose Cannabinoids.
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying a supportive compression bandage or brace before attempting any movement, you will artificially increase the proprioceptive feedback to your brain, directly reducing the risk of further subluxations and rewarding yourself with safe mobility.
Home-Based Services Optional: Referral to hypermobility-aware physiotherapist.
DME/Safety Optional: Provision of slings/braces for acute stabilization.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to coordinate allied health management for chronic instability.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if a joint slips out and visibly stays out of place, if a limb becomes suddenly cold or blue, or if numbness develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on clinical exam; occult labral or meniscal tears from the subluxation cannot be ruled out without MRI.
Clinical Course: The acute muscle spasm and micro-trauma inflammation will subside over 3-7 days with rest and anti-inflammatories. The underlying collagen defect remains a lifelong management issue.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 or go to the ED immediately if your shoulder or knee pops out and you cannot get it back in, or if your hand or foot turns cold and blue.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your muscles have gone into a severe cramp to try and hold your loose joints together; the medication relaxes these muscles and reduces the swelling from the micro-tears.
Actionable Steps: Avoid stretching or extreme ranges of motion for the next 72 hours; keeping your joints in a neutral, mid-range position allows the overstretched ligaments to rest, positively reinforcing structural stability.

